course: dictionary
lesson: 12044
title: Circumnavigate: all the way around
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some long words are built from smaller parts. Circum means around, and navigate means to steer a ship, plane or boat, so circumnavigate means to travel all the way around something. «The crew wanted to circumnavigate the island in one week.» The clue is all the way around: the crew sails in a big circle and comes back to where it started.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. «The fallen tree blocked the path, so we circumnavigated it through the field.» You cannot go through a tree, so you go around it. Here circumnavigate means finding a way around a problem or obstacle.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in an easier phrase. In the first sentence, "sailed all the way around" fits. In the second, "went around" fits. If your meaning makes the sentence sensible, you are probably right.
fact: Circumnavigate is a verb, so it can change: circumnavigates, circumnavigated, circumnavigating.
fact: «Circumnavigate» means to sail, fly or travel all the way around something, especially the world or an island.
fact: «Circumnavigate» can also mean to find a way around a problem or obstacle.
fact: Explorers such as Ferdinand Magellan's crew sailed around the world between 1519 and 1522.
fact: Common phrases are «circumnavigate the globe» and «circumnavigate the island».
exercise: «The sailors set off to circumnavigate the globe.» What does circumnavigate mean here? | To paint a picture of the sea | To sink under the waves | *To sail all the way around the world | To stay safely in the port
exercise: «The fallen tree blocked the path, so we circumnavigated it.» What did we do? | *We went around it to get past | We cut the tree down with a saw | We waited for the tree to move | We climbed on top and stopped
exercise: Which phrase means to travel all the way around an island? | Swim to the middle of the lake | Dig a hole in the island | Stay on the beach | *Circumnavigate the island
test: What does the start of the word, circum, mean? | Never | *Around | Under | Before
test: «We circumnavigated the lake in our canoe.» Which phrase means the same as circumnavigated? | *Sailed all the way around | Dived under the water | Crossed the lake quickly | Ran away from the lake
exam: «The explorers circumnavigated the world.» Which answer uses the clues best? | Circum means under, so they went underground | *Circum means around, so they travelled all the way around the world | Circum means happy, so they were pleased | Circum means fast, so they raced
